Target bypass
❖ Bypass the drug target:
❑ Target becomes redundant by acquisition of an alternative enzyme;
❑ Replace drug target by an alternative target;
❑ Overproduce drug target to consume the drug.
Nat. Rev. Microbiol., 2023, 21(5)

Basic principles
❖ Antibiotics should be used to treat some types of bacterial infection：
❑ Diagnosis: identify the pathogenic bacteria AEAP;
❑ Narrow spectrum: preferentially selecting antibiotics that target
specific bacterial species rather than broad-spectrum antibiotics;
❑ Empiric therapy: initiating antibiotic treatment based on clinical
suspicion while awaiting culture results;
❑ Dosage: appropriate dose to achieve therapeutic level in the body;
❑ Duration: shortest effective duration to achieve clinical;
❑ Preventing cross-transmission: minimize the spread of antibiotic-
resistant bacteria;
❑ Education: the appropriate use of antibiotics, AMR, and the
importance of adherence to treatment regimens;
❑ Surveillance: monitoring antibiotic use and resistance patterns to
guide antimicrobial stewardship efforts and identify emerging
resistance trends;
❑ Prevention use: avoid, except in operation or post-exposure.

Considering factors
❖ Bacteria:
❑ Antibiotic sensitivity and resistance.
❖ Antibiotics:
❑ PK, PD, toxicity, side-effects, administration routes;
❑ Dose-dependent (high dose, less times);
❑ Time-dependent (low dose, multiple times).
❖ Patients:
❑ Patient conditions (severity, site of infection);
❑ Patient factors (age, comorbidities, allergies, renal and hepatic
function, pregnancy, and potential drug interactions);
❑ Patient references (income, beliefs, ability to adhere to treatment);
❖ Cost and availability: the cost of antibiotics, accessibility, local law and
restrictions.

Special topics – Drug combination
❖ If a single drug is sufficient, no need combination therapy;
❖ Four classes of antibiotics:
❑ Class I: bactericidal @ exponential phase (β-Lactams);
❑ Class II: bactericidal @ stationary phase (Aminoglycosides);
❑ Class III: bacteriostatic, fast (Macrolides);
❑ Class IV: bacteriostatic, slow (Sulfonamides);
▪ Class I + II: good choice (β-Lactams destroy cell wall is good for
uptake of aminoglycosides);
▪ Class I + III: bad choice (macrolides inhibit cell viability to reduce
PG synthesis);
▪ Class III + IV: good choice (inhibit protein synthesis and
metabolism pathway at the same time).

Antiviral strategies
❖ Antiviral drugs:
❑ Inhibit but not “kill”;
❑ ~90 FDA-approved drugs;
❑ High side-effects;
C&EN, 2022

Inhibitors for DNA/RNA synthesis
❖ Nucleoside analogues:
❑ Adefovir 阿德福韦 (adenosine analog): for HBV;
❑ Tenofovir 替诺福韦 (deoxyadenosine analog): for HIV, HBV;
❑ Ribavirin 利巴韦林 (guanosine analog): for HCV, hemorrhagic fevers;
❑ Acyclovir 阿昔洛韦 (guanosine analog): for HSV;
❑ Entecavir 恩替卡韦 (deoxyguanosine analog): for HIV, HBV;
❑ Zidovudine 齐多夫定 (thymidine analog): for HIV;
❑ Telbivudine 替比夫定 (thymidine analog): for HBV;
❑ Lamivudine 拉米夫定 (cytidine analog): for HIV, HBV;
❑ Sofosbuvir 索非布韦 (uridine analog): for HCV;
